00:45:10.096 --> 00:45:14.096
(speaking in foreign language)

00:45:16.470 --> 00:45:18.402
- Like just again and again and again

00:45:18.402 --> 00:45:19.476
so it just stuck.
- Right.

00:45:19.476 --> 00:45:20.459
- While I'm doing that.

00:45:20.459 --> 00:45:22.285
So then got burned out on that.

00:45:22.285 --> 00:45:23.364
Came to Japan.

00:45:23.364 --> 00:45:25.568
I wanted to do anime stuff and car stuff.

00:45:25.568 --> 00:45:26.401
- Right.

00:45:26.401 --> 00:45:27.407
- How did you come here?

00:45:27.407 --> 00:45:28.450
Like what was the process like?

00:45:28.450 --> 00:45:29.283
Did you get like a-

00:45:29.283 --> 00:45:30.263
- No, I just did tourist.

00:45:30.263 --> 00:45:31.579
- Oh, just tourist.

00:45:31.579 --> 00:45:33.223
- Yeah, just as a tourist.

00:45:33.223 --> 00:45:35.630
I wanted to see car stuff,
I wanted to see anime stuff.

00:45:35.630 --> 00:45:37.990
So I went to Akihabara.

00:45:37.990 --> 00:45:40.540
It was all in a notebook, all written out.

00:45:40.540 --> 00:45:42.495
No Google Maps. None of this.

00:45:42.495 --> 00:45:43.740
I didn't know what I was looking for.

00:45:43.740 --> 00:45:44.978
- So what year was this?

00:45:44.978 --> 00:45:47.496
- This was like 2005.

00:45:47.496 --> 00:45:50.427
- Oh, fuck.
- Oh my goodness.

00:45:50.427 --> 00:45:52.716
- I was blind.
- Yeah.

00:45:52.716 --> 00:45:55.430
- I had two friends had been to Japan.

00:45:55.430 --> 00:45:56.683
One who'd done car stuff.

00:45:56.683 --> 00:45:59.475
One who'd done, he'd sort
of gone back to Akihabara.

00:45:59.475 --> 00:46:01.834
So he's like drawing me
hand-drawn maps of like,

00:46:01.834 --> 00:46:03.740
"Okay, so this is the LAOX building,

00:46:03.740 --> 00:46:05.170
and if you turn this
way, there's the bridge

00:46:05.170 --> 00:46:06.003
and you walk down that way,

00:46:06.003 --> 00:46:06.836
'cause that's where all the stuff is."

00:46:06.836 --> 00:46:07.669
Like okay.

00:46:08.780 --> 00:46:10.040
'Cause I didn't have
any friends who wanted

00:46:10.040 --> 00:46:12.830
to do car stuff and
anime stuff so by myself.

00:46:12.830 --> 00:46:15.090
So, you've done the same thing, I'm sure.

00:46:15.090 --> 00:46:16.530
You burn yourself out.

00:46:16.530 --> 00:46:17.690
You get up in the morning

00:46:17.690 --> 00:46:18.980
and go do stuff all day.

00:46:18.980 --> 00:46:20.070
You don't eat, you don't drink.

00:46:20.070 --> 00:46:21.466
You just do more of this sort of stuff.

00:46:21.466 --> 00:46:22.637
You go to the hotel fall asleep, wake up,

00:46:22.637 --> 00:46:23.660
let's do it again!

00:46:23.660 --> 00:46:28.180
So I go out again and went up
to Ebisu Circuit by myself.

00:46:28.180 --> 00:46:30.637
- And you went all the way
to Fukushima by yourself?

00:46:30.637 --> 00:46:33.510
- Drove there, or?
- No, I took the train,

00:46:33.510 --> 00:46:37.040
I was blind, a friend of mine
who worked at a magazine,

00:46:37.040 --> 00:46:38.450
actually was a friend of a friend,

00:46:38.450 --> 00:46:40.470
he worked at a magazine and I just said,

00:46:40.470 --> 00:46:41.653
he sorta knew who I was.

00:46:41.653 --> 00:46:43.003
So I messaged him like,

00:46:43.003 --> 00:46:44.331
well, I emailed him.

00:46:44.331 --> 00:46:45.514
And said, "Hey, can you tell me

00:46:45.514 --> 00:46:46.365
how to get to Ebisu Circuit?"

00:46:46.365 --> 00:46:48.000
He said, "Just go to Nihonmatsu Station

00:46:48.000 --> 00:46:51.000
and just get in a taxi and
just say Ebisu Circuit."

00:46:51.000 --> 00:46:52.178
(all laughing)

00:46:52.178 --> 00:46:53.635
He said "Oh, are you going there?"

00:46:53.635 --> 00:46:54.993
I'm like, "Yeah."

00:46:54.993 --> 00:46:56.290
"Oh, can you write an article

00:46:56.290 --> 00:46:59.004
about the event for the magazine?"

00:46:59.004 --> 00:46:59.868
"Yeah."

00:46:59.868 --> 00:47:03.035
So I've got my mum's little Panasonic,

00:47:03.035 --> 00:47:07.467
like digital camera, 32
megabyte memory card,

00:47:07.467 --> 00:47:08.837
Not gigabyte, megabyte.

00:47:08.837 --> 00:47:10.538
- Megabyte.
- Right?

00:47:10.538 --> 00:47:13.630
So I go up there, I see the
event, just blows my mind,

00:47:13.630 --> 00:47:15.600
all this cool stuff, it
was a big competition,

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:17.322
which I now do the commentary for,

00:47:17.322 --> 00:47:19.038
which is kind of cool.
- Yeah.

00:47:19.038 --> 00:47:20.400
- Full circle.

00:47:20.400 --> 00:47:22.457
- Go back, I write this
article and he's like,

00:47:22.457 --> 00:47:23.450
"Oh, that was really good."

00:47:23.450 --> 00:47:25.627
And then a few weeks, you
know about a month later,

00:47:25.627 --> 00:47:26.925
he was like, "Do you want a job?"

00:47:26.925 --> 00:47:28.593
"Yes, I'm sick of doing 3D, I hate it."

00:47:28.593 --> 00:47:31.020
So, I started working at the magazine

00:47:31.020 --> 00:47:34.427
and every chance I could, I come to Japan.

00:47:34.427 --> 00:47:35.635
And this is how we did it.

00:47:35.635 --> 00:47:37.218
This is how, these people have to remember

00:47:37.218 --> 00:47:38.351
this sort of stuff.

00:47:38.351 --> 00:47:41.329
You somehow make money when you come here.

00:47:41.329 --> 00:47:42.453
- Right.
- Yeah.

00:47:42.453 --> 00:47:43.286
- If you can.

00:47:43.286 --> 00:47:45.210
Like people do that by
like making YouTube videos

00:47:45.210 --> 00:47:46.110
and stuff like that.

00:47:46.110 --> 00:47:47.217
So what I did was I would come here,

00:47:47.217 --> 00:47:48.660
I'd go do car stuff.

00:47:48.660 --> 00:47:50.148
I'd take photos of it, write articles,

00:47:50.148 --> 00:47:53.461
go back and I'd get paid
for going on holiday.

00:47:53.461 --> 00:47:54.577
Which is so fun.

00:47:54.577 --> 00:47:55.976
- It's a double win.

00:47:55.976 --> 00:47:58.170
- Yeah, it's like this is perfect.

00:47:58.170 --> 00:47:59.828
- I have gamed the system.

00:47:59.828 --> 00:48:00.813
(laughing)

00:48:00.813 --> 00:48:02.630
- So I thought okay,
I got this figured out

00:48:02.630 --> 00:48:05.158
and it got to the point
where literally everyone

00:48:05.158 --> 00:48:06.737
I knew it was like,

00:48:06.737 --> 00:48:09.232
"Lexi, just shut the fuck
up and go live there."

00:48:09.232 --> 00:48:10.529
(all laughing)

00:48:10.529 --> 00:48:12.145
That's all you talk about.

00:48:12.145 --> 00:48:14.653
My car friends, everyone's like "Alexi,

00:48:14.653 --> 00:48:17.888
Japan this, well in Japan,
well in Japan they do this,

00:48:17.888 --> 00:48:19.337
in Japan they do that, so just go."

00:48:19.337 --> 00:48:20.987
I said, "Okay, fine, I will."

00:48:20.987 --> 00:48:22.300
So working holiday visa, right?

00:48:22.300 --> 00:48:23.966
- Right.
- 18 months, okay.

00:48:23.966 --> 00:48:27.195
I'm gonna quit everything,
sell everything.

00:48:27.195 --> 00:48:28.590
I'm gonna live there
for a year and a half.

00:48:28.590 --> 00:48:30.353
If it works out, if
I've had a good holiday,

00:48:30.353 --> 00:48:32.201
I can just shut up about it
and get it out of my system.

00:48:32.201 --> 00:48:33.930
If not, we'll see what happens.

00:48:33.930 --> 00:48:36.750
So came here and just did the same thing,

00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:40.463
just went around shooting
articles, writing things,

00:48:40.463 --> 00:48:41.630
selling them to magazines,

00:48:41.630 --> 00:48:43.770
selling them all around
the world in English.

00:48:43.770 --> 00:48:45.210
And it just sort of built up from there.

00:48:45.210 --> 00:48:46.817
So like taking photos.

00:48:46.817 --> 00:48:48.204
Oh, look at these cool random photos

00:48:48.204 --> 00:48:49.700
I've taken of this cool stuff.

00:48:49.700 --> 00:48:52.339
I'll make a WordPress blog,

00:48:52.339 --> 00:48:53.853
which is what everybody did back then,

00:48:53.853 --> 00:48:55.236
this was like 2008.

00:48:55.236 --> 00:48:56.595
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:48:56.595 --> 00:48:57.901
- Okay, WordPress blog.

00:48:57.901 --> 00:49:00.348
So that became a popular thing.

00:49:00.348 --> 00:49:02.580
Then, oh, this is really cool,

00:49:02.580 --> 00:49:05.670
I've got this little Sony vloggy camera.

00:49:05.670 --> 00:49:06.520
It only shoots-

00:49:06.520 --> 00:49:08.524
- I think I have the
exact same camera, yeah.

00:49:08.524 --> 00:49:10.525
- So like oh, let's just shoot this car

00:49:10.525 --> 00:49:12.936
doing something cool, 'cause it looks,

00:49:12.936 --> 00:49:14.903
it's moving, it's making noise.

00:49:14.903 --> 00:49:15.963
Here we go.

00:49:15.963 --> 00:49:17.397
- Cars do that.

00:49:17.397 --> 00:49:18.710
- That's what makes 'em cool.

00:49:18.710 --> 00:49:21.487
- Was it the old Sony Handycams?

00:49:21.487 --> 00:49:22.897
- It was a tiny little one.

00:49:22.897 --> 00:49:24.480
- Oh, okay, okay.

00:49:24.480 --> 00:49:25.765
So shoot all that.

00:49:25.765 --> 00:49:27.430
And then that goes on YouTube.

00:49:27.430 --> 00:49:29.200
Well, that becomes more popular.

00:49:29.200 --> 00:49:30.760
You know, magazines aren't popular.

00:49:30.760 --> 00:49:31.960
They don't make any money anymore.

00:49:31.960 --> 00:49:34.110
They don't want to pay $800,000

00:49:34.110 --> 00:49:36.080
for a big article these days anymore.

00:49:36.080 --> 00:49:36.913
Okay.

00:49:36.913 --> 00:49:38.390
YouTube pays money, let's do YouTube.

00:49:38.390 --> 00:49:39.960
And it's just sort of
snowballed from there.

00:49:39.960 --> 00:49:43.410
- So how did you transition
from doing all the online stuff

00:49:43.410 --> 00:49:45.780
into then actually
living here permanently?

00:49:45.780 --> 00:49:48.010
'Cause I assume you had to
figure out a way, right?

00:49:48.010 --> 00:49:50.267
- Yeah well, and this is one
of the biggest questions,

00:49:50.267 --> 00:49:51.133
everyone wants to know like,

00:49:51.133 --> 00:49:51.966
"How do you live in Japan?"

00:49:51.966 --> 00:49:53.650
- 'Cause we get it all the time as well.

00:49:53.650 --> 00:49:55.067
- I'm sure, yeah.

00:49:57.387 --> 00:50:00.333
It's a difficult thing because
there's so many people,

00:50:00.333 --> 00:50:01.870
I'm sure you've had this same experience.

00:50:01.870 --> 00:50:02.900
Like people who are living here,

00:50:02.900 --> 00:50:05.070
it's like, "How are you living here?"

00:50:05.070 --> 00:50:08.253
Like you don't, you have to do something.

00:50:08.253 --> 00:50:10.060
- It's like what do you do?

00:50:10.060 --> 00:50:12.537
- I have some people where I'm like, how?

00:50:12.537 --> 00:50:15.364
- It's like what visa do you have?

00:50:15.364 --> 00:50:18.553
How have you coined, how
have you gamed the system

00:50:18.553 --> 00:50:19.810
in your own way, right?

00:50:19.810 --> 00:50:20.950
- And it's sort of like,

00:50:20.950 --> 00:50:23.891
you're not supposed to ask that question.

00:50:23.891 --> 00:50:26.765
Like what visa and how are you here?

00:50:26.765 --> 00:50:30.106
'Cause I mean, look, some people just,

00:50:30.106 --> 00:50:32.640
they're lucky like, "Oh
my, my father is Japanese,

00:50:32.640 --> 00:50:34.153
I got a visa."

00:50:34.153 --> 00:50:35.917
Or not lucky, you know what I mean?

00:50:35.917 --> 00:50:37.442
Like almost like it's a bad thing.

00:50:37.442 --> 00:50:38.684
- Yeah.

00:50:38.684 --> 00:50:42.041
- Or for some reason they're here.

00:50:42.041 --> 00:50:44.863
So the way I did it was...

00:50:44.863 --> 00:50:46.800
So originally working holiday visa.

00:50:46.800 --> 00:50:48.990
And then when that started to run out was,

00:50:48.990 --> 00:50:50.690
I thought, okay, I need to do something.

00:50:50.690 --> 00:50:55.690
And I had a friend who lived up in Sendai,

00:50:55.880 --> 00:50:58.750
who was a French guy and he was
actually running a magazine,

00:50:58.750 --> 00:50:59.710
a French magazine.

00:50:59.710 --> 00:51:03.380
He was running the magazine
as the editor from Japan.

00:51:03.380 --> 00:51:05.780
So he was just getting all
this content from Japan,

00:51:05.780 --> 00:51:06.970
just editing it.

00:51:06.970 --> 00:51:09.630
And so he was on a journalist visa.

00:51:09.630 --> 00:51:10.608
- Oh, okay.

00:51:10.608 --> 00:51:11.927
- So I thought he said,

00:51:11.927 --> 00:51:13.380
"Oh, you should do the journalist visa.

00:51:13.380 --> 00:51:14.376
It's very easy."

00:51:14.376 --> 00:51:15.982
(all laughing)

00:51:15.982 --> 00:51:17.998
Sorry, JC, that's a bad imitation of you.

00:51:17.998 --> 00:51:19.610
(all laughing)

00:51:19.610 --> 00:51:21.730
So I thought, okay, maybe I can do this.

00:51:21.730 --> 00:51:23.180
So I applied for everything.

00:51:23.180 --> 00:51:24.540
Now, remember when the
earthquake happened,

00:51:24.540 --> 00:51:25.653
oh, you weren't even here
when the earthquake happened.

00:51:25.653 --> 00:51:26.715
The earthquake-
- 2011?

00:51:26.715 --> 00:51:27.631
- Yeah.

00:51:27.631 --> 00:51:29.578
The earthquake happened

00:51:29.578 --> 00:51:33.223
and I'd already sort of renewed once

00:51:33.223 --> 00:51:34.620
and it worked and I thought,

00:51:34.620 --> 00:51:37.112
okay, I don't know if I can do this again.

00:51:37.112 --> 00:51:38.830
It was kind of easy the first time,

00:51:38.830 --> 00:51:43.830
because I managed to get like
a contract from a magazine

00:51:43.892 --> 00:51:47.210
and I don't know if it would
work these days or not,

00:51:47.210 --> 00:51:48.543
but I had proof

00:51:48.543 --> 00:51:50.470
that I'd been doing that
job for a long time.

00:51:50.470 --> 00:51:52.010
And the best thing
about being a journalist

00:51:52.010 --> 00:51:54.483
is you don't need any sort of-

00:51:54.483 --> 00:51:56.074
- Like qualification?
- Yeah, you don't.

00:51:56.074 --> 00:51:57.190
All you need is like,

00:51:57.190 --> 00:51:58.615
I've done this for a long time.

00:51:58.615 --> 00:51:59.448
- I'm a keep doing.

00:51:59.448 --> 00:52:01.030
- Yeah, I'm a keep doing it.

00:52:01.030 --> 00:52:02.252
These guys are gonna pay me to do it.

00:52:02.252 --> 00:52:04.140
Okay, so I sort of convinced

00:52:04.140 --> 00:52:05.100
a magazine to give me a contract.

00:52:05.100 --> 00:52:06.829
Like, look, you want this content?

00:52:06.829 --> 00:52:08.178
Give me a contract.

00:52:08.178 --> 00:52:09.210
- [Connor] Yeah.
- Right, okay.

00:52:09.210 --> 00:52:11.360
- So I got that, I showed it to them.

00:52:11.360 --> 00:52:16.250
Then again, that whole
earthquake thing happened.

00:52:16.250 --> 00:52:17.827
And this was like
literally at the same time

00:52:17.827 --> 00:52:19.130
I was meant to renew.

00:52:19.130 --> 00:52:22.157
I thought, "Oh no, what
if they cancel me or?"

00:52:22.157 --> 00:52:23.323
- Right.
- So, I mean, look,

00:52:23.323 --> 00:52:24.731
everyone, I don't know about you guys-

00:52:24.731 --> 00:52:26.173
- 'Cause then it's like
panic state, right?

00:52:26.173 --> 00:52:27.473
- Dude, it's all-

00:52:27.473 --> 00:52:28.770
- Yeah, what was that like?

00:52:28.770 --> 00:52:29.603
(Alexi sighs)

00:52:29.603 --> 00:52:30.436
- Okay.

00:52:30.436 --> 00:52:31.827
When I...

00:52:31.827 --> 00:52:35.620
It wasn't bad for me because
all I did was just sort of sit

00:52:35.620 --> 00:52:38.043
at home for like a month
and just do nothing.

00:52:38.043 --> 00:52:39.850
I just thought I'm just gonna
stay the hell out of it.

00:52:39.850 --> 00:52:40.683
- Yeah.

00:52:40.683 --> 00:52:41.610
- Until I need to do something.

00:52:41.610 --> 00:52:46.027
Actually, I did go and help
a friend who was at Ebisu

00:52:46.027 --> 00:52:48.400
who had a lot of trouble with
stuff falling down the hill,

00:52:48.400 --> 00:52:50.110
like collapsing, anyway.

00:52:50.110 --> 00:52:50.943
- Right, right, right.

00:52:50.943 --> 00:52:54.533
- So I thought, okay,
I don't want to leave.

00:52:54.533 --> 00:52:57.699
If I leave now, probably
not gonna come back.

00:52:57.699 --> 00:52:59.879
'Cause a lot of, even hear of flyjin,

00:52:59.879 --> 00:53:01.603
you heard that term?
- No.

00:53:01.603 --> 00:53:04.230
- So foreigners who left Japan

00:53:04.230 --> 00:53:06.916
when the earthquake happened
and was like, I'm out.

00:53:06.916 --> 00:53:09.030
(all laughing)
- Flyjin!

00:53:09.030 --> 00:53:10.646
- Was that a real thing?

00:53:10.646 --> 00:53:11.860
A lot of foreigners left?

00:53:11.860 --> 00:53:13.930
- Yeah, well, okay, it was
kind of derogatory thing.

00:53:13.930 --> 00:53:15.640
It's like, "Yeah, you left."

00:53:15.640 --> 00:53:17.248
It's like, I honestly don't
blame people who did that

00:53:17.248 --> 00:53:18.196
'cause it was-
- Was it that bad?

00:53:18.196 --> 00:53:19.529
- Understandable, have a nice day.

00:53:19.529 --> 00:53:20.554
- I see footage of it,

00:53:20.554 --> 00:53:22.733
but it's so hard to like
actually get a sense of it

00:53:22.733 --> 00:53:23.822
because even the small earthquakes here

00:53:23.822 --> 00:53:25.690
kind of like throw me off sometimes.

00:53:25.690 --> 00:53:26.874
So I can only imagine what an earthquake

00:53:26.874 --> 00:53:28.149
of that magnitude would do.

00:53:28.149 --> 00:53:30.840
- I don't know, I don't think
people can quote me on this,

00:53:30.840 --> 00:53:33.430
but wasn't it one of the
most like, financially,

00:53:33.430 --> 00:53:36.280
it was one of the most like
devastating earthquakes,

00:53:36.280 --> 00:53:38.517
slash tsunamis of all time or something?

00:53:38.517 --> 00:53:41.659
- Well, probably the deadliest,
not the deadliest but-

00:53:41.659 --> 00:53:43.160
- The one that did the most damage.

00:53:43.160 --> 00:53:44.430
- Yeah, I think like it caused

00:53:44.430 --> 00:53:46.170
the most damage financially.

00:53:46.170 --> 00:53:48.100
- It did cause a lot of problems.

00:53:48.100 --> 00:53:50.120
- Really fucked up the Japanese economy.

00:53:50.120 --> 00:53:52.530
- Depending on how severe I hear it was

00:53:52.530 --> 00:53:55.290
is depending on who I ask and
where they were at the time.

00:53:55.290 --> 00:53:57.497
Because some people said like,
I've been told this like,

00:53:57.497 --> 00:53:59.837
"It wasn't that bad, things
just started falling."

00:53:59.837 --> 00:54:01.130
- No, it was bad. (laughing)

00:54:01.130 --> 00:54:02.630
- But that's what I'm saying, right?

00:54:02.630 --> 00:54:04.592
Depends on who you ask and
where they were at the time.

00:54:04.592 --> 00:54:05.425
- Right.

00:54:05.425 --> 00:54:06.616
- Because obviously if
someone experienced it

00:54:06.616 --> 00:54:07.449
and it wasn't bad for them,

00:54:07.449 --> 00:54:08.310
they probably think like eh.

00:54:08.310 --> 00:54:11.931
- I mean, it was on like,
every country it was on news.

00:54:11.931 --> 00:54:14.590
- Like it was fucking worldwide news.

00:54:14.590 --> 00:54:16.320
- No, I know, I'm not saying it's not.

00:54:16.320 --> 00:54:18.340
what I'm asking is like,
how was it for like you,

00:54:18.340 --> 00:54:19.890
because everyone I've seemed to ask so far

00:54:19.890 --> 00:54:22.110
has such a different explanation
of how it was for them.

00:54:22.110 --> 00:54:24.180
- Personally, I mean,
when it actually happened,

00:54:24.180 --> 00:54:27.460
I was just doing some washing at the time

00:54:27.460 --> 00:54:29.170
'cause it was like in the afternoon.

00:54:29.170 --> 00:54:30.486
And I thought, oh, I'm gotta take it down

00:54:30.486 --> 00:54:32.739
to the laundromat and get the dryer going.

00:54:32.739 --> 00:54:35.380
And some thing I was gonna hang up as well

00:54:35.380 --> 00:54:37.530
and the windows started rattling.

00:54:37.530 --> 00:54:39.180
I'm like, oh, it's windy.

00:54:39.180 --> 00:54:40.435
Good, I can get my-

00:54:40.435 --> 00:54:42.246
(all laughing)

00:54:42.246 --> 00:54:44.058
- So I can wash this.

00:54:44.058 --> 00:54:45.437
- I can hang my sheets
up, get them to dry.

00:54:45.437 --> 00:54:47.397
And then all of a sudden
everything started,

00:54:47.397 --> 00:54:49.527
I was on the second floor of a house

00:54:49.527 --> 00:54:51.420
that I used to live in Saitama.

00:54:51.420 --> 00:54:54.975
And it started to move, I'm
like oh earthquake, okay.

00:54:54.975 --> 00:54:56.000
What do I do?

00:54:56.000 --> 00:54:58.150
Okay, stand in the, so I
stood in the doorframe.

00:54:58.150 --> 00:54:58.983
- Right.
- That's what you're supposed

00:54:58.983 --> 00:55:00.133
to do I guess.

00:55:00.133 --> 00:55:01.100
- I think that's what
you're supposed to do.

00:55:01.100 --> 00:55:03.742
- And it started to, it's just doing this.

00:55:03.742 --> 00:55:06.090
And I'm like okay, this is bad, wait,

00:55:06.090 --> 00:55:07.580
I'm supposed to be a media guy.

00:55:07.580 --> 00:55:08.612
Where's my camera?

00:55:08.612 --> 00:55:10.800
So I grabbed my vloggy again

00:55:10.800 --> 00:55:13.502
and I caught the last like
20 seconds of it on video.

00:55:13.502 --> 00:55:15.212
And I aimed out the window.

00:55:15.212 --> 00:55:19.767
All the power lines are
doing this and it's rattling.

00:55:19.767 --> 00:55:21.400
And it's just crazy.

00:55:21.400 --> 00:55:22.450
And I remember it like that too

00:55:22.450 --> 00:55:23.930
and then it just kind of stopped

00:55:23.930 --> 00:55:25.810
and there was a guy doing a delivery,

00:55:25.810 --> 00:55:27.390
like (speaking in foreign
language) guy of the road

00:55:27.390 --> 00:55:29.050
and he just kept doing the (indistinct).

00:55:29.050 --> 00:55:30.166
(Garnt laughs)

00:55:30.166 --> 00:55:31.240
- [Joey] Yeah, like nothing happened.

00:55:31.240 --> 00:55:32.330
- Is anything gonna happen?

00:55:32.330 --> 00:55:34.330
And I'm looking at,
there's no smoke rising.

00:55:34.330 --> 00:55:36.117
It's okay, it should be okay.

00:55:36.117 --> 00:55:38.889
And so I put my stuff in my laundry thing,

00:55:38.889 --> 00:55:40.027
went down to the laundromat,

00:55:40.027 --> 00:55:42.962
and I'm in there, just sitting
there waiting for it to dry,

00:55:42.962 --> 00:55:45.170
I was like, okay, I'll go
shopping maybe in a minute.

00:55:45.170 --> 00:55:47.549
And these kids walk
past the window outside

00:55:47.549 --> 00:55:49.132
wearing those hats,

00:55:50.282 --> 00:55:51.782
you know the foam?

00:55:52.700 --> 00:55:54.956
Yeah, it's like this kind of-

00:55:54.956 --> 00:55:56.364
- It's like a triangular-

00:55:56.364 --> 00:55:58.010
- It's like a bandana
looking thing that they wear

00:55:58.010 --> 00:55:59.450
and it's supposed to be fireproof?

00:55:59.450 --> 00:56:00.283
- Yeah.
- Right.

00:56:00.283 --> 00:56:01.997
- And like elementary school kids wear it,

00:56:01.997 --> 00:56:03.510
to like kind of protect themselves-

00:56:03.510 --> 00:56:05.310
- It's like a Red Riding Hood hood,

00:56:05.310 --> 00:56:08.160
but it's like thick
foam made of reflective,

00:56:08.160 --> 00:56:09.710
like an old fireman's hat sort of thing?

00:56:09.710 --> 00:56:10.755
- My God yeah, in the UK,

00:56:10.755 --> 00:56:12.532
they're like fucking get on with it.

00:56:12.532 --> 00:56:13.365
(Joey laughs)

00:56:13.365 --> 00:56:14.810
- Well, I mean, there's
nothing that happens in the UK

00:56:14.810 --> 00:56:16.600
that's big enough to be
worried about, right?

00:56:16.600 --> 00:56:18.054
- They will walk past
the window and I'm like,

00:56:18.054 --> 00:56:19.164
What the hell are these?

00:56:19.164 --> 00:56:20.220
Oh, they're wearing those things, okay.

00:56:20.220 --> 00:56:23.540
And then in the laundromat,
sometimes there's a TV.

00:56:23.540 --> 00:56:26.145
And I look up and there's
that the big black wall

00:56:26.145 --> 00:56:28.247
of death just eating towns.

00:56:28.247 --> 00:56:29.164
I'm like...

00:56:30.362 --> 00:56:31.613
(Garnt laughs)

00:56:31.613 --> 00:56:33.852
Oh, oh shit, I better go get some,

00:56:33.852 --> 00:56:36.330
I better go and buy
some food or something.

00:56:36.330 --> 00:56:39.112
So I went to the supermarket
and just bought a bunch of food

00:56:39.112 --> 00:56:42.582
and I thought okay, and a
slab of beer and I thought...

00:56:42.582 --> 00:56:43.782
(all laughing)

00:56:43.782 --> 00:56:45.498
- Might be a long night.

00:56:45.498 --> 00:56:47.350
- Well I thought if I'm
gonna be stuck at home.

00:56:47.350 --> 00:56:49.610
And yeah, so it was okay for me.

00:56:49.610 --> 00:56:52.912
The worst thing for doing car
content was there was no fuel.

00:56:52.912 --> 00:56:54.648
They just cut, all the fuel got cut off.

00:56:54.648 --> 00:56:55.747
So I was like, okay.

00:56:55.747 --> 00:56:56.971
And what I actually did that month was

00:56:56.971 --> 00:56:59.000
I had enough of a internet
presence then to say,

00:56:59.000 --> 00:57:00.416
'cause I had a regular column

00:57:00.416 --> 00:57:01.692
in a Japanese magazine actually.

00:57:01.692 --> 00:57:02.900
And I said, "Hey, everyone,

00:57:02.900 --> 00:57:05.153
send me a picture of
your foreign drift car."

00:57:05.153 --> 00:57:08.382
And I got people to
make my content for me.

00:57:08.382 --> 00:57:10.935
- You're a pro YouTuber.
(all laughing)

00:57:10.935 --> 00:57:12.093
God damn.

00:57:12.093 --> 00:57:17.080
- But up in Tohoku like up
where, especially where Ebisu is,

00:57:17.080 --> 00:57:19.330
I had to go up there
to help a friend just-

00:57:19.330 --> 00:57:21.940
- So you weren't in Sendai
when it actually happened?

00:57:21.940 --> 00:57:23.507
- No, no, no, I wasn't up there.

00:57:23.507 --> 00:57:24.963
- 'Cause my grandma lives in Sendai.

00:57:24.963 --> 00:57:25.796
So she like-

00:57:25.796 --> 00:57:27.532
- Yeah, 'cause what I've
heard is that from people

00:57:27.532 --> 00:57:28.940
who were in Sendai near the epicenter,

00:57:28.940 --> 00:57:29.773
it was awful,

00:57:29.773 --> 00:57:31.137
but then I've heard a lot of
people in Tokyo who were like,

00:57:31.137 --> 00:57:32.350
"Yeah, things were shaking."

00:57:32.350 --> 00:57:33.808
- Yeah, I mean, for my grandma,

00:57:33.808 --> 00:57:35.370
it was kind of like half-half,

00:57:35.370 --> 00:57:39.940
because like she lives on quite
a big like apartment place.

00:57:39.940 --> 00:57:42.133
I think she was on like the
13th or 14th floor or something.

00:57:42.133 --> 00:57:43.904
And like obviously the higher up

00:57:43.904 --> 00:57:45.404
on a building you are, right,

00:57:45.404 --> 00:57:46.935
the more it's gonna shake.

00:57:46.935 --> 00:57:49.434
So the shaking was bad but
it didn't break anything.

00:57:49.434 --> 00:57:51.297
Like she didn't get injured or whatever,

00:57:51.297 --> 00:57:52.820
but then she did tell me that

00:57:52.820 --> 00:57:56.078
it was really scary that after
the rattling kind of stopped

00:57:56.078 --> 00:57:58.710
and she picked herself back
up and looked out the window,

00:57:58.710 --> 00:58:01.580
she could actually see tsunami
coming over the horizon.

00:58:01.580 --> 00:58:02.580
- Oh my God.

00:58:03.452 --> 00:58:05.480
- And then she was like, "Oh, Shit."

00:58:05.480 --> 00:58:07.118
And so she turned the TV on

00:58:07.118 --> 00:58:09.519
and then that really famous footage

00:58:09.519 --> 00:58:11.457
of the black wall was just-

00:58:11.457 --> 00:58:13.130
- Wait, what is the black wall?

00:58:13.130 --> 00:58:14.963
- I just made that up.

00:58:16.130 --> 00:58:18.572
The tsunami was just like a big churning,

00:58:18.572 --> 00:58:20.223
dark mess of debris.

00:58:20.223 --> 00:58:22.090
- It wasn't like this one big wave

00:58:22.090 --> 00:58:23.237
that most people think it is.

00:58:23.237 --> 00:58:24.970
It was kind of just this rush of water

00:58:24.970 --> 00:58:26.506
that just swept over everything.

00:58:26.506 --> 00:58:30.360
- Because it was just taking
all the debris with it, right?

00:58:30.360 --> 00:58:31.703
So, it was just like,

00:58:31.703 --> 00:58:34.270
it just turned a black
wall of like destruction

00:58:34.270 --> 00:58:37.025
because it was hardly water anymore.

00:58:37.025 --> 00:58:38.615
'Cause it was just like all the gunk

00:58:38.615 --> 00:58:39.825
that it'd taken up with it.

00:58:39.825 --> 00:58:41.600
- And just all the houses that were along

00:58:41.600 --> 00:58:43.020
the coast were just getting crushed

00:58:43.020 --> 00:58:45.230
as this thing came over
and yeah, it was horrible.

00:58:45.230 --> 00:58:47.062
- Meanwhile, international
people like were like,

00:58:47.062 --> 00:58:48.878
"But my anime."

00:58:48.878 --> 00:58:51.051
- I remember, like Pokemon
got delayed or something?

00:58:51.051 --> 00:58:52.535
- Madoka got delayed.

00:58:52.535 --> 00:58:53.627
- And people were like,

00:58:53.627 --> 00:58:55.720
"I can't believe this
is stopping the anime."

00:58:55.720 --> 00:58:57.069
It's like, oh my God.

00:58:57.069 --> 00:58:58.802
- It's like fuck you. (laughing)

00:58:58.802 --> 00:59:01.230
- And trains were canceled for a while.

00:59:01.230 --> 00:59:02.760
And they had rolling blackouts.

00:59:02.760 --> 00:59:03.894
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:59:03.894 --> 00:59:05.530
- Luckily 'cause I was,

00:59:05.530 --> 00:59:08.080
the grid that I was on at
my house was the same grid

00:59:08.080 --> 00:59:11.075
as a big hospital and a
Japanese military base

00:59:11.075 --> 00:59:12.513
so we never got a cut.

00:59:12.513 --> 00:59:13.696
But here's the thing.

00:59:13.696 --> 00:59:16.545
So what I was saying before
was I had to renew my visa.

00:59:16.545 --> 00:59:19.687
And at that point you
had to get a reentry visa

00:59:19.687 --> 00:59:21.220
to come back into the country.

00:59:21.220 --> 00:59:23.685
Which means you have to
go to the visa center,

00:59:23.685 --> 00:59:27.000
get like a $20 stamp in your
thing, in your passport,

00:59:27.000 --> 00:59:28.310
so if you leave the country,

00:59:28.310 --> 00:59:29.700
you can come back and everyone's leaving,

00:59:29.700 --> 00:59:33.425
'cause it's like, well there's
radiation landing on Tokyo.

00:59:33.425 --> 00:59:34.768
I mean, understandably.

00:59:34.768 --> 00:59:37.210
So, and I was just, I thought okay, fine.

00:59:37.210 --> 00:59:39.010
But I was like trying to renew
my thing at the same time.

00:59:39.010 --> 00:59:42.085
So I went to the visa center
at like three in the afternoon,

00:59:42.085 --> 00:59:43.242
which was a bad idea.

00:59:43.242 --> 00:59:45.870
I drove there, out in Saitama.

00:59:45.870 --> 00:59:49.959
And the queue was about
300 meters long of people.

00:59:49.959 --> 00:59:51.367
I thought-
- What is this, Anime Expo?

00:59:51.367 --> 00:59:52.707
(all laughing)

00:59:52.707 --> 00:59:54.560
- I thought okay, here's
what I have to do.

00:59:54.560 --> 00:59:56.480
Okay, I have to come
back tomorrow morning,

00:59:56.480 --> 00:59:58.070
before the first train.

00:59:58.070 --> 01:00:00.503
So my car, because my
car ran on premium fuel,

01:00:00.503 --> 01:00:01.920
this is a drift car.

01:00:01.920 --> 01:00:02.753
No fuel.
- Right.

01:00:02.753 --> 01:00:03.980
(Garnt laughs)

01:00:03.980 --> 01:00:06.396
- I like how you had to
mention premium fuel.

01:00:06.396 --> 01:00:08.504
- Just reminding you, I'm a drift channel.

01:00:08.504 --> 01:00:09.962
- It was a drift car.

01:00:09.962 --> 01:00:12.082
- So I begged my friend, like,

01:00:12.082 --> 01:00:13.470
please lend me your little Kei car,

01:00:13.470 --> 01:00:14.620
your little small compact car

01:00:14.620 --> 01:00:16.260
so I can drive there
before the first train

01:00:16.260 --> 01:00:17.093
so I can get in line.

01:00:17.093 --> 01:00:18.222
Get there, there's already about

01:00:18.222 --> 01:00:20.090
maybe 50 meters of people lined up.

01:00:20.090 --> 01:00:20.923
- Oh my God.

01:00:20.923 --> 01:00:22.400
- I didn't get in the front door until,

01:00:22.400 --> 01:00:24.637
I lined up like maybe 4:30 in the morning.

01:00:24.637 --> 01:00:28.865
Didn't get in the front door
until two in the afternoon.

01:00:28.865 --> 01:00:30.697
And then as soon as I
got in the front door,

01:00:30.697 --> 01:00:35.142
everyone's going to the left,
I go to the right to renew.

01:00:35.142 --> 01:00:36.526
And I walk up, there's
no one at the counter,

01:00:36.526 --> 01:00:37.525
'cause they're all busy over here.

01:00:37.525 --> 01:00:38.442
I'm like...

01:00:39.420 --> 01:00:41.629
And so someone walks past is like,

01:00:41.629 --> 01:00:42.777
"What do you want?"

01:00:42.777 --> 01:00:43.858
"Oh, I want to renew."

01:00:43.858 --> 01:00:45.356
And they're like, "Oh, okay."

01:00:45.356 --> 01:00:46.189
They just take it.

01:00:46.189 --> 01:00:47.620
And they sort of just
flicked through and like,

01:00:47.620 --> 01:00:49.214
"You got your tax thing in there?"

01:00:49.214 --> 01:00:50.874
I was like, "Oh, I forgot that."

01:00:50.874 --> 01:00:52.041
It's like, "Eh, don't worry about it."

01:00:52.041 --> 01:00:53.402
(all laughing)

01:00:53.402 --> 01:00:54.633
- They just couldn't be bothered.

01:00:54.633 --> 01:00:56.402
- They couldn't be
bothered, I got the renewal.

01:00:56.402 --> 01:00:57.485
It was great.

01:00:57.485 --> 01:00:59.565
And then just as I went back, I was like,

01:00:59.565 --> 01:01:00.952
"Oh, that's over.

01:01:00.952 --> 01:01:02.318
Thank God for that, I can go home."

01:01:02.318 --> 01:01:03.676
Went to the car park to get my car,

01:01:03.676 --> 01:01:06.555
it was a coin parking
with the little gates.

01:01:06.555 --> 01:01:10.840
So I get in the car, go to
drive out and go to the gate,

01:01:10.840 --> 01:01:12.775
put my money in the machine.

01:01:12.775 --> 01:01:15.747
All I had was $100 like a
10,000 yen, like $100 bill.

01:01:15.747 --> 01:01:16.947
You can put them in the machines here,

01:01:16.947 --> 01:01:20.038
put it in the machine, the gates open

01:01:20.038 --> 01:01:22.705
and it's like where's my change?

01:01:23.727 --> 01:01:25.876
Four o'clock scheduled blackout.

01:01:25.876 --> 01:01:27.125
- Oh no!
- Oh my God.

01:01:27.125 --> 01:01:28.700
- So the machine ate
my hundred dollar bill.

01:01:28.700 --> 01:01:29.593
I'm like, oh.

01:01:29.593 --> 01:01:30.880
(all laughing)

01:01:30.880 --> 01:01:33.870
Yeah, I look around and all
the lights are blanked out

01:01:33.870 --> 01:01:35.086
and people are coming out of the bank

01:01:35.086 --> 01:01:36.210
because it's just got blacked out.

01:01:36.210 --> 01:01:37.043
I'm like oh no.

01:01:37.043 --> 01:01:39.780
So anyway, I called up
and this being Japan,

01:01:39.780 --> 01:01:41.010
there was a very lovely girl.

01:01:41.010 --> 01:01:43.315
And she's like, answering
the phone and like,

01:01:43.315 --> 01:01:45.340
"I'm sorry, the machine
ate my hundred dollars."

01:01:45.340 --> 01:01:46.806
She's like, "Oh, don't worry.

01:01:46.806 --> 01:01:47.779
(speaking in foreign language)

01:01:47.779 --> 01:01:50.623
We'll make sure it's okay
and we'll send you the money.

01:01:50.623 --> 01:01:52.040
We'll get you the
information of your bank."

01:01:52.040 --> 01:01:53.413
Sure enough like a week
later, they did it.

01:01:53.413 --> 01:01:55.050
- I mean, Japan's the one country

01:01:55.050 --> 01:01:55.994
where you probably wouldn't mind

01:01:55.994 --> 01:01:57.618
a machine eating your money.
- Yeah.

01:01:57.618 --> 01:01:59.338
- You know you were getting that back.

01:01:59.338 --> 01:02:00.479
- You can get it back, yeah, exactly.

01:02:00.479 --> 01:02:02.112
- So, that was a thing.

01:02:02.112 --> 01:02:04.098
So since then, but now
I'm married with kids

01:02:04.098 --> 01:02:05.316
and I'm an adult now.

01:02:05.316 --> 01:02:06.633
- [Connor] Gosh, terrifying.

01:02:06.633 --> 01:02:07.466
- A proper adult.

01:02:07.466 --> 01:02:08.749
- What's that like?

01:02:08.749 --> 01:02:09.964
(chuckles)

01:02:09.964 --> 01:02:11.289
- I gotta ask though,

01:02:11.289 --> 01:02:13.262
because you came to Japan

01:02:13.262 --> 01:02:14.909
obviously much earlier than us.

01:02:14.909 --> 01:02:16.920
'Cause for us coming to Japan,

01:02:16.920 --> 01:02:19.880
there's plenty of resources
for us to learn about

01:02:19.880 --> 01:02:21.537
what Japan life is like.

01:02:21.537 --> 01:02:23.900
We have like a lot of, way more vloggers

01:02:23.900 --> 01:02:25.035
and more YouTube videos

01:02:25.035 --> 01:02:26.690
and more resources that we can look at.

01:02:26.690 --> 01:02:27.790
But what was it like for you?

01:02:27.790 --> 01:02:29.490
Was it almost like coming in blind?

01:02:29.490 --> 01:02:33.220
Like what was it like
coming to Japan back then,

01:02:33.220 --> 01:02:35.170
compared to how it is now?

01:02:35.170 --> 01:02:36.988
- The first time I came, oh, I'm sorry.

01:02:36.988 --> 01:02:38.725
The time I moved here,

01:02:38.725 --> 01:02:41.965
I already been here
about six or seven times,

01:02:41.965 --> 01:02:43.856
- [Connor] God damn.

01:02:43.856 --> 01:02:46.248
- Mostly doing work or
like what I said before

01:02:46.248 --> 01:02:50.498
like I go on holiday and
so I had a lot of friends.

01:02:51.790 --> 01:02:53.933
Make friends with people
who speak English and stuff.

01:02:53.933 --> 01:02:55.936
You know, I'd have floors to sleep on

01:02:55.936 --> 01:02:57.896
and contacts and things like that,

01:02:57.896 --> 01:03:00.356
which with Japanese people,
they're very friendly.

01:03:00.356 --> 01:03:03.043
You can impose on the
Japanese person terribly

